-- the six pairs, in order:
--
-- alpha- and beta-D-glucopyranose. Different InChIs, different constructions, and in water one substance:
-- the interconversion class whose composition at 25 C is 36 to 64, which is what a bottle labelled
-- D-glucose contains. (false, false, true)
--
-- (R,R)-tartaric acid and meso-tartaric acid. Different InChIs, different constructions, and different
-- substances -- different melting points, different solubilities, different behaviour in every chiral
-- environment. (false, false, false)
--
-- a keto and an enol form of one compound, under standard InChI. The identifier was intended from the
-- early 2000s to be tautomer-invariant and implements that invariance only partially, so for the
-- tautomeric pairs it does cover the two records carry one identifier while the constructions differ and
-- the substance is one. (true, false, true)
--
-- ritonavir Form I and Form II. The same molecule, so the same InChI and the same molecular construction;
-- not the same substance. Form II is more stable and much less soluble, was not bioequivalent in the
-- marketed dosage forms, and took the capsule off the market in 1998. (true, true, false)
--
-- an InChIKey collision pair: two different structures with the same key, which the key's designers state
-- is unavoidable and whose rate they publish -- about 1.3% chance of a first-block collision in a database
-- of a billion compounds, with the first collision reported in 2009. Same digest, different constructions,
-- different substances. (true, false, false)
--
-- and one record against itself, so that the tallies below are counts over a list containing the trivial
-- case rather than a list selected to prove a point. (true, true, true)
record-pairs : List Trio
record-pairs = cons (false , false , true) (cons (false , false , false) (cons (true , false , true) (cons (true , true , false) (cons (true , false , false) (cons (true , true , true) nil)))))
